INT. DRAINED PUBLIC SWIMMING POOL - NIGHT

Empty deep end. Cracked blue tiles. A single work light on a stand. Rain taps the skylights.

CASPIAN "CAZ" REYES-MORROW, 40s, rumpled suit, sits on the pool ladder, briefcase on his lap. Waiting.

Footsteps echo above. A woman descends the shallow-end stairs, hands visible.

LUDMILA "LUDDY" KOVAC, 30s, leather jacket, calm to the point of insult.

LUDDY
Caz.

CAZ
Luddy. You're early.

LUDDY
You're sweating.

CAZ
It's a pool. Humidity.

LUDDY
It's empty.

She tilts her head at the briefcase. He tightens his grip.

LUDDY (CONT'D)
Where's Tomas?

CAZ
Parking.

LUDDY
Tomas doesn't drive.

A beat. The rain gets louder. Somewhere a pipe knocks.

CAZ
He learned.

LUDDY
Caz.

CAZ
Luddy, don't.

She walks the slope of the pool floor, slow, boots squeaking on old algae. Caz stands, the ladder clanking, and sets the briefcase between them on the tile. He steps back. She does not step forward. Above, a shadow slides across the skylight and is gone, and Caz looks up while Luddy looks at him.

LUDDY (CONT'D)
That's not Tomas.

CAZ
It's the wind.

LUDDY
It's Dutch Maren. I can smell the cigarettes.

CAZ
Luddy, I had no choice.

LUDDY
Everyone says that at the end.

A metal door bangs open above. Heavy steps. A voice, thick and amused, rolls down the tiles.

TOMAS (O.S.)
Caz, my friend. You were supposed to be quiet.

Caz drops. Luddy draws a pistol from her jacket, spins, fires at the light stand. Glass bursts. Darkness, except the faint blue of the skylights. Caz crawls behind the ladder, hugging the briefcase, while Luddy slides down the wall of the deep end and Tomas' boots clatter on the steps, unhurried, a man descending to a party.

TOMAS (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Luddy. You always did love a dramatic entrance.

LUDDY
(whisper)
Caz, open the case.

CAZ
There's nothing in it.

LUDDY
What?

CAZ
There was never anything in it!

Tomas laughs in the dark. A lighter flicks. A small flame shows his face at the lip of the pool, smiling.
